# Individual Detail Page Architecture - Server Components + React Query Pattern

## Overview

Individual detail pages in Yardli use a sophisticated hybrid architecture combining Next.js 15 server components with React Query for optimal performance and user experience. This pattern provides server-side rendering benefits while maintaining rich client-side interactivity.

## Core Architecture Principles

### 1. Server-First with Client Enhancement

- **Server Components**: Primary data fetching and initial rendering
- **Client Components**: Interactive features, mutations, and real-time updates
- **Hybrid Pattern**: Server components provide initial data, client components enhance with React Query

### 2. State Management Strategy

- **Jotai Atoms**: Global state management with SSR hydration
- **React Query**: Client-side data fetching, caching, and synchronization
- **Initial Data Atoms**: Bridge between server and client state

## Folder Structure Pattern

```
src/app/{entity}/[id]/
├── page.tsx                    # Server component - main layout & data fetching
├── loading.tsx                 # Loading UI for entire page
└── components/
    ├── {Entity}Header.tsx      # Client component - navigation & actions
    ├── {Entity}Info.tsx        # Server component - static entity data
    ├── {Entity}InfoLoading.tsx # Loading UI for entity info
    ├── {Entity}Jobs/
    │   ├── index.tsx          # Server component - data fetching
    │   └── table.tsx          # Client component - interactive table
    ├── JobsLoading.tsx        # Loading UI for jobs section
    ├── {Entity}Estimates/
    │   ├── index.tsx          # Server component - data fetching
    │   └── table.tsx          # Client component - interactive table
    ├── EstimatesLoading.tsx   # Loading UI for estimates section
    └── Delete{Entity}.tsx     # Client component - destructive actions
```

## Server Component Pattern (page.tsx)

```typescript
import React, { Suspense } from "react";
import { notFound } from "next/navigation";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";
import { Container, Stack, Divider } from "@mantine/core";
import { HydrationBoundary } from "jotai-ssr";
import { initialEntityDetailAtom } from "@/lib/atoms";

export default async function Index(props: {
    params: Promise<{ id: string }>;
}) {
    const params = await props.params;

    // Server-side data fetching
    const entity = await prisma.entity.findUnique({
        where: { id: params.id },
    });

    if (!entity) {
        return notFound();
    }

    return (
        <Container size="lg" py="md">
            <Stack gap="lg">
                <HydrationBoundary
                    hydrateAtoms={[[initialEntityDetailAtom, entity]]}
                >
                    <EntityHeader entity={entity} />

                    <Suspense fallback={<EntityInfoLoading />}>
                        <EntityInfo {...entity} />
                    </Suspense>

                    <Divider />

                    <Suspense fallback={<JobsLoading />}>
                        <EntityJobs entityId={params.id} />
                    </Suspense>

                    <Divider />

                    <Suspense fallback={<EstimatesLoading />}>
                        <EntityEstimates entityId={params.id} />
                    </Suspense>

                    <DeleteEntity id={params.id} />
                </HydrationBoundary>
            </Stack>
        </Container>
    );
}
```

## Server Component Pattern (Data Fetching)

```typescript
// Server component - async function, direct Prisma calls
export async function EntityJobs({ entityId }: { entityId: string }) {
    const jobs = await prisma.job.findMany({
        where: { entityId },
        include: {
            entity: {
                select: { id: true, name: true },
            },
        },
        orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" },
    });

    return <EntityJobsTable jobs={jobs as Job[]} />;
}
```

## Client Component Pattern (Interactive Features)

```typescript
"use client";

export const EntityJobsTable = ({ jobs: initialJobs }: { jobs: Job[] }) => {
    const entity = useAtomValue(initialEntityDetailAtom);
    const queryClient = useQueryClient();

    // React Query with initial data from server
    const { data: jobs } = useQuery({
        queryKey: ["entity", "jobs", entity.id],
        queryFn: () => fetchJSON<Job[]>(`/api/jobs/entity/${entity.id}`),
        enabled: !!entity.id,
        initialData: initialJobs, // Server data as initial state
    });

    // Optimistic mutations
    const deleteJobMutation = useMutation({
        mutationFn: (id: string) =>
            fetchJSON<Job>(`/api/jobs/${id}`, { method: "DELETE" }),
        onMutate: async (jobId) => {
            // Cancel outgoing refetches
            await queryClient.cancelQueries({
                queryKey: ["entity", entity.id],
            });

            // Snapshot previous value
            const previousEntity = queryClient.getQueryData([
                "entity",
                entity.id,
            ]);

            // Optimistically update
            queryClient.setQueryData(["entity", entity.id], (old: any) => {
                if (!old) return old;
                return {
                    ...old,
                    jobs:
                        old.jobs?.filter((job: Job) => job.id !== jobId) || [],
                };
            });

            return { previousEntity };
        },
        onError: (err, jobId, context) => {
            // Rollback on error
            if (context?.previousEntity) {
                queryClient.setQueryData(
                    ["entity", entity.id],
                    context.previousEntity
                );
            }
            notifications.show({
                title: "Error",
                message: "Failed to delete job",
                color: "red",
            });
        },
        onSuccess: () => {
            notifications.show({
                title: "Success",
                message: "Job deleted successfully",
                color: "green",
            });
            queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["entity", entity.id] });
        },
    });

    return (
        <Stack gap="md">
            <Group justify="space-between" align="center">
                <Title order={4}>Jobs ({jobs.length})</Title>
                <JobsClient
                    entityId={entity.id}
                    refetchQueryKeys={[["entity", "jobs", entity.id]]}
                />
            </Group>

            {jobs.length === 0 ? (
                <Text c="dimmed" ta="center" py="md">
                    No jobs found for this entity.
                </Text>
            ) : (
                <div
                    style={{
                        overflowX: "auto",
                        WebkitOverflowScrolling: "touch",
                    }}
                >
                    <Table
                        highlightOnHover
                        withRowBorders={false}
                        verticalSpacing="sm"
                    >
                        {/* Table content */}
                    </Table>
                </div>
            )}
        </Stack>
    );
};
```

## Loading States Pattern

### Three-Tier Loading Strategy

1. **Page-Level Loading** (`loading.tsx`)

```typescript
export default function Loading() {
    return (
        <Container size="lg" py="md">
            <Stack gap="lg">
                {/* Header skeleton */}
                <Group justify="space-between" align="center">
                    <Skeleton height={32} width={200} />
                    <Skeleton height={36} width={80} />
                </Group>

                {/* Entity info skeleton */}
                <Stack gap="md">
                    <Skeleton height={24} width={150} />
                    <Stack gap="sm">
                        <Skeleton height={36} />
                        <Skeleton height={36} />
                        <Skeleton height={36} />
                    </Stack>
                </Stack>

                <Divider />

                {/* Related data sections skeleton */}
                {/* ... */}
            </Stack>
        </Container>
    );
}
```

2. **Section-Level Loading** (e.g., `JobsLoading.tsx`)

```typescript
export function JobsLoading() {
    return (
        <Stack gap="md">
            <Group justify="space-between" align="center">
                <Skeleton height={24} width={100} />
                <Skeleton height={32} width={80} />
            </Group>
            <Stack gap="xs">
                <Skeleton height={48} />
                <Skeleton height={48} />
                <Skeleton height={48} />
            </Stack>
        </Stack>
    );
}
```

## Data Flow Architecture

### 1. Initial Page Load (SSR)

```
Server Component (page.tsx)
├── Fetches entity data via Prisma
├── Hydrates Jotai atoms with initial data
├── Renders server components with data
└── Suspense boundaries for async sections
```

### 2. Client-Side Enhancement

```
Client Components
├── Receive initial data from server components
├── Use React Query with initialData
├── Handle user interactions and mutations
└── Sync with server via API routes
```

### 3. State Synchronization

```
Jotai Atoms (Global State)
├── initialEntityDetailAtom - Entity data
├── Hydrated on server, consumed on client
└── Shared across components

React Query (Data Fetching)
├── Query keys: ["entity", "jobs", entityId]
├── Initial data from server components
├── Background refetching and caching
└── Optimistic updates for mutations
```

## Type Safety Patterns

### Shared Types (in /src/lib/atoms.ts)

```typescript
export type Entity = {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    email?: string | null;
    phone?: string | null;
    address?: string | null;
    notes?: string | null;
    createdAt: string | Date;
};

export type Job = {
    id: string;
    title: string;
    date?: string | Date | null;
    status: string;
    createdAt: string | Date;
    entity?: Omit<Entity, "createdAt">;
    // ... additional fields
};
```

### Type Casting for Prisma

```typescript
// Server components cast Prisma results to client types
return <EntityJobsTable jobs={jobs as Job[]} />;

// Handle serialization for complex objects
return (
    <EntityEstimatesTable
        estimates={JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(estimates)) as Estimate[]}
    />
);
```

## Query Key Patterns

```typescript
// Hierarchical query keys for React Query
["entity", "jobs", entityId][("entity", "estimates", entityId)][
    ("entity", entityId)
]; // For entity data
```

## Component Composition Rules

### When to Use Server Components

- Initial data fetching
- Static content rendering
- SEO-critical content
- Performance-critical data loading

### When to Use Client Components

- User interactions (forms, buttons, modals)
- Real-time updates
- Complex state management
- Browser APIs usage

### File Naming Conventions

- Server components: PascalCase (e.g., `EntityInfo.tsx`)
- Client components: PascalCase with "use client"
- Loading components: `[Section]Loading.tsx`
- Table components: `[Entity]Table.tsx`

## Best Practices

1. **Server-First**: Always start with server components for data fetching
2. **Hydration**: Use HydrationBoundary to bridge server and client state
3. **Suspense**: Wrap async server components in Suspense boundaries
4. **Optimistic Updates**: Implement rollback mechanisms for mutations
5. **Error Handling**: Graceful fallbacks for server and client errors
6. **Type Safety**: Consistent typing between server and client
7. **Performance**: Initial data eliminates loading states
8. **Caching**: Strategic use of React Query caching with proper keys

## Common Utility Functions

### Date Formatting

```typescript
function formatDate(dateString?: string | Date | null) {
    if (!dateString) return "—";
    return new Date(dateString).toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
        year: "numeric",
        month: "short",
        day: "numeric",
        hour: "2-digit",
        minute: "2-digit",
    });
}
```

### Status Colors

```typescript
function getStatusColor(status: string) {
    switch (status.toLowerCase()) {
        case "scheduled":
            return "blue";
        case "in_progress":
            return "yellow";
        case "completed":
            return "green";
        case "cancelled":
            return "red";
        default:
            return "gray";
    }
}
```

### Delete Confirmation

```typescript
const handleDelete = (id: string) => {
    modals.openConfirmModal({
        title: "Delete Entity?",
        children: (
            <Text size="sm">
                Are you sure you want to delete this entity? This action cannot
                be undone.
            </Text>
        ),
        labels: { confirm: "Delete", cancel: "Cancel" },
        confirmProps: { color: "red" },
        onConfirm: () => deleteMutation.mutate(id),
    });
};
```

This architecture provides optimal performance through server-side rendering while maintaining rich client-side interactivity and real-time updates.
